# NVIDIA UVM device nodes — create /dev/nvidia-uvm{,-tools} at boot.
#
# The nvidia_uvm module is loaded at boot (the driver's modprobe.d carries
# `softdep nvidia post: nvidia-uvm`), but loading it does NOT create its
# device nodes: nothing in the RPM Fusion packaging does, for UVM. They
# only appear the first time something calls nvidia-modprobe -u, and the
# usual something is nvidia-smi. Until then CUDA cannot initialise, and
# with it NGX -- so DLSS through dxvk-nvapi fails, and games that probe
# for it at startup (KCD2, The Witcher 3 remastered) refuse to launch
# with a GPU-incompatibility error. Running nvidia-smi once after boot
# "fixed" it for exactly that reason.
#
# nvidia-modprobe rather than a hand-rolled mknod: it reads the dynamic
# major from /proc/devices itself (it is not fixed -- 507 here, but it
# moves with the set of char drivers loaded), and it is what nvidia-smi
# runs under the hood, so the nodes come out identical, 0666 root:root.
# -c0 also makes sure /dev/nvidia0 exists, which is a no-op when the
# modeset driver already made it.
#
# SUBSYSTEM=="module" fires when the module is loaded, and coldplug
# (systemd-udev-trigger replays "add" for everything at boot) replays it
# if the module was loaded before udev's rules were read.
#
# Installed by scripts/install-hardware.sh (the hardware unit) on any
# machine with an NVIDIA GPU, whether or not the driver is in yet: until
# it is, there is no nvidia_uvm to load and the rule never matches.
ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="module", KERNEL=="nvidia_uvm", \
  RUN+="/usr/bin/nvidia-modprobe -c0 -u"
